{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "https://schema.org/"
  },
  "@type": "Note",
  "@id": "note:notion-44",
  "title": "Dynamic Views | Open Questions",
  "author": "person:jane-brew:001",
  "status": "draft",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "b-1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Dynamic Views | Open Questions"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Functional Details"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-3",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. **Behavior of the \"board\" view**:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Should it mimic a kanban-style interface (as implied by the presence of `Kanban.vue`)? If so, what specific interactions or behaviors should be standardized across pages (e.g., drag-and-drop, column management)?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-5",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> Yes, it will behave exactly like Linear or Trello."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-6",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-7",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Are there any features specific to the \"board\" view that diverge from the existing views?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-8",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> Not really. All views are just different ways to represent the same data, kinda like how it works in Notion."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-9",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-10",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. **Customization Scope**:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-11",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- What kind of props will users typically configure for each view? For instance, are we talking about display fields, sorting, filtering, or more specific UI behaviors?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-12",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> Definitely filtering/sorting. I think it would be cool to allow the user to pick which properties to display as well (with some defaults in place, and a way for the user to reset the properties to default)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-13",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-14",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Should these props be explicitly defined per page, or should `@PageWrapper.vue` offer a default set of props that individual pages can extend/override?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-15",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> They should be based on the shape of the data coming in… we can assume name, id, created_at, and updated_at exist for all entities. for instance, if the users data looks like this: `{ id: String, name: String, email: String, age: Number, status: String, description: String }`, the default properties might be [email, age, status], but the user can toggle the visibility of `description` if they'd like, or any other prop for that matter. This will require a default design for each item that includes each prop that is consistent for every view, and every page."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-16",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-17",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. **Data Handling**:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-18",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- How should the individual pages provide data to `@PageWrapper.vue`? Should it just be a single array/object, or do we need to account for more complex schemas (e.g., paginated data, nested structures)?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-19",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> A single JSON prop. (ie. `<PageWrapper :data=\"dataFromApi\" />` where `dataFromApi` is JSON, or an array of objects). The properties, filter options etc should be dynamic based on the shape of the JSON"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-20",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-21",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Is there a shared API contract for how data flows between pages and views?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-22",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> Would love your help formally defining the API contract based on my responses."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-23",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-24",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "4. **Slot Content Use Cases**:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-25",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Are the slotted content use cases purely for layouts and modals, or do you envision other dynamic elements being passed? Should we consider slots for things like custom toolbars or filters?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-26",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> Slotted content is for the following:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-27",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-28",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> - Dialog modals for the primary action dialog modal: eg. slotted wysiwyg for creating new note, slotted form for inviting a new member, slotted form + inputs for tags and searchable dropdowns for creating tasks like linear.app, etc"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-29",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> - Custom item layouts (optional for developer): The layout for items within views and their properties should be consistent across all pages. If a page requires a custom layout for each item (eg. notes having a content preview included in the grid view cards), we should be able to include that as a slot for any view. I imagine we’d need to use named slots for this… We’ll also need to be highly intentional about what the default layout looks like so it can be as general/agnostic as possible for most cases and most kinds of data."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-30",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Will modals (e.g., \"new item\" or \"edit item\") be unique per page, or should `@PageWrapper.vue` also provide standardized modals that pages can extend?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-31",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> Same deal. Named slots with agnostically designed defaults."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-32",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-33",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-34",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Technical Concerns"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-35",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. **Existing Code**:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-36",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- From reviewing `@Tasks.vue`, `Kanban.vue`, and `@PageWrapper.vue`, are there specific pain points or inconsistencies we’re trying to resolve? For example:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-37",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Do views currently have repetitive code for rendering and handling data?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-38",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> Yes, right now the main pages (members, shows, channels, tasks, notes) use a copy of the same layout and functionality, so its difficult to keep everything consistent. I created PageWrapper.vue to solve this problem, keep things dry, and make each page only responsibly for providing the data and passing the appropriate props."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-39",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-40",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Are props already being passed inconsistently across pages?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-41",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> Yes things are inconsistent right now but the PageWrapper aims to make things more dry."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-42",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-43",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Do modals or interactions require duplication of logic between pages?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-44",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> They do, that’s something I want to dry throughout the app."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-45",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-46",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. **Responsiveness & Theming**:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-47",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Should each view (grid, list, table, board) be responsive by default? Are there specific breakpoints or design rules we should enforce?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-48",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> If by responsive you mean mobile friendly then yes, absolutely."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-49",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-50",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Do we need to accommodate any global theming or customization that may impact view layouts?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-51",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> We should always use shadcn, or custom blocks made of shadcn components."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-52",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-53",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-54",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Future-Proofing"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-55",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. **Scalability**:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-56",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Do you anticipate adding more views beyond grid, list, table, and board? If so, should we design `@PageWrapper.vue` to make it easier to slot in new views in the future?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-57",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> I would like for users to be able to create custom views… this might a feature to include down the line so not a top priority."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-58",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-59",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-60",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> For we only have grid view, list view, table view, and board view. I’d like to also include a calendar and timeline/gantt view."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-61",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-62",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Should we build view logic in a modular way so that new pages can integrate without heavy coupling?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-63",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> Can you elaborate on this please? I’m interested in what you mean by this."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-64",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-65",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. **Edge Cases**:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-66",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- How should `@PageWrapper.vue` handle cases where a page doesn’t define slotted content or specific props? Should there be fallbacks for those scenarios?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-67",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> Absolutely. It’ll be important to make the defaults/fallbacks as agnostic as possible (still working to define what that means)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-68",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-69",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Are there pages that will require exceptions to this standardization (e.g., unique layouts or interactions)?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-70",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> yes, but in those cases I probably will just not use PageWrapper."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-71",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-72",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-73",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Philosophical/Strategic"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-74",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. **End-User Control**:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-75",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- How much freedom do you want to give users? For example, do we want users to save their view configurations (e.g., saving a grid view with specific filters for future use)?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-76",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> Maybe so… it’s a tricky question and something I’ve been wrestling with. This is essentially a web app tailored for a specific niche and target audience so it shouldn’t be as open ended as something like Notion, but at the same time I’d like to make it as open ended as Notion so I can make it as customizable/configurable as possible. That’s really important to me… As a tool that is not open source, I want users to be able to tailor the uI to fit their unique needs. End user programming is something that is very important to me as well, so things like formula props, advanced filtering, and custom dashboards is something I’d like to add at some point. The user should have as much control as possible."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-77",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-78",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Should the UI allow toggling between views easily, or is this something configured per page?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-79",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> easy toggling between views for all PageWrapper routes. The view options available should be something specified as a prop to <PageWrapper />, defaulting to all view options available."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-80",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-81",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. **Alignment with Notion**:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-82",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- When you say \"kinda like how it works in Notion,\" which aspects of Notion’s behavior are most crucial to replicate? (e.g., flexibility, interactivity, data relationships, or something else?)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-83",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> All of that. Especially flexibility & data relationships. All the pages are connected, so when they create a new task for example, they might choose a project from a dropdown that pulls data from the ‘projects’ page… everything is organized as a graph of entities. (see attached diagram for reference)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-84",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-85",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-86",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "```markdown"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-87",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Board View"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-90",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-88",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Behavior**: Kanban-style interface like Linear/Trello"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-89",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Features**: Same as other views - just a different representation of data (like Notion)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-91",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Customization"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-93",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-92",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Properties**:"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-94",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Default filtering/sorting capabilities"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-95",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Configurable display properties with defaults"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-96",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Properties based on data shape"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-97",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Common fields: name, id, created_at, updated_at"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-98",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Example: For user data {id, name, email, age, status, description}:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-99",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
